  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhancing Protection of Persons of Concern in Mexico, Guatemala, El Salvador, Honduras and Costa Rica through Humanitarian/Shelter Assistance, Protection Assistance, Cash-Based Interventions and Improved Coordination among Actors</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Costa Rica experiences a mix of migratory movements to and through the country. According to the Displacement Tracking Matrix Report conducted between October 2021 and March 2022, 51 per cent of migrants in transit in Costa Rica lacked economic resources; 42 per cent report a need for food and water; 34 per cent did not have a place to sleep; and 17 per cent report a need for security along their route. Despite the recent increase of persons in mixed movements entering Costa Rica the Migration Authority does not offer any temporary shelter options, neither at the southern nor at the northern border. This situation forces people to continue their journey to San Jose, where there are very few options for humanitarian shelter which often results in family groups and individuals living in homeless situations. Through this program, IOM is working to enhance protection and humanitarian solutions, including shelter, for vulnerable migrants and asylum seekers transiting through Costa Rica.</narrative>
  </description>
